# Project: Jest JavaScript/TypeScript Unit Test Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript and TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or alongside source files with .test.ts/.spec.ts suffixes
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest or Babel for type-safe test implementations
- Leverage Jest's built-in assertion library and rich ecosystem

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices
- Use meaningful test and variable naming that reads like plain English
- Implement clean code principles with single-responsibility tests
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks with clear, descriptive names
- Use it/test functions for individual test cases
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach for setup and teardown; beforeAll/afterAll sparingly
- Separate unit, integration, and snapshot tests into distinct directories

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern for every test
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests that cover one behavior
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state
- Use jest.fn() for mocking instead of manual mock implementations
- Prefer testing behavior over implementation details

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for module-level mocking with automatic hoisting
- Implement jest.spyOn() for method-level spying without replacing originals
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() for time-dependent tests (setTimeout, Date)
- Mock HTTP requests with msw (Mock Service Worker) or jest.mock() for fetch/axios
- Reset mocks in afterEach using jest.clearAllMocks() or configure clearMocks: true globally

## Assertions
- Use expect() with specific, descriptive matchers
- Implement custom matchers via expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions
- Use toMatchSnapshot() and toMatchInlineSnapshot() for stable output testing
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality over toBe for object/array comparisons
- Use expect.assertions(n) for async tests to ensure callbacks are called

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with --coverage flag or jest.config collectCoverage
- Set meaningful coverage thresholds in jest.config for statements, branches, functions, and lines
- Use coveragePathIgnorePatterns to exclude generated, vendor, or non-testable code
- Generate lcov and html reports; upload lcov to Codecov or SonarQube in CI

## Performance
- Run test suites in parallel using Jest's default worker pool
- Use --runInBand only for debugging or when tests share global resources
- Implement proper test isolation to enable safe parallel execution
- Use testPathPattern or --testNamePattern to run focused subsets during development

## Component Testing
- Integrate with React Testing Library (@testing-library/react) for user-centric assertions
- Use @testing-library/user-event for realistic user interaction simulation
- Configure jsdom as the test environment for DOM-dependent tests
- Avoid testing internal component state; test visible behavior and output

## Snapshot Testing
- Use snapshots for stable, presentational UI components and serialized structures
- Keep snapshots small and focused; large snapshots become brittle
- Commit snapshot files to version control alongside test files
- Review snapshot diffs carefully in code review; update only when change is intentional

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or fixtures for reusable, composable test data
- Define TypeScript interfaces or types for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants or data builders
- Generate dynamic or random data with @faker-js/faker for fuzz-style tests

## CI/CD Integration
- Use --ci flag in CI environments to fail on new snapshots and disable interactive mode
- Configure --reporter=default,jest-junit for CI pipeline compatibility
- Cache the Jest cache directory (jest --cacheDirectory) across pipeline runs
- Run tests with --forceExit to prevent hanging workers in CI environments

## Environment Configuration
- Use jest.config.ts (TypeScript config) for type-checked configuration
- Configure testEnvironment per file with @jest-environment docblock for mixed projects
- Handle environment variables with dotenv or jest's globalSetup/globalTeardown
- Use setupFilesAfterFramework (setupFilesAfterFramework) for global matchers and polyfills

## Error Handling
- Test both success and failure code paths for all public functions
- Assert on specific error messages and error types using expect().toThrow()
- Use expect().rejects.toThrow() for async error assertions with proper awaiting
- Test boundary conditions: null, undefined, empty arrays, and edge-case inputs

## TypeScript Integration
- Use ts-jest or @swc/jest for fast TypeScript compilation in tests
- Configure paths in tsconfig to match Jest's moduleNameMapper for alias resolution
- Use jest.Mocked<T> and jest.MockedFunction<T> generic types for type-safe mocks
- Enable strict mode in TypeScript to catch type errors in test files

## Documentation
- Document complex test scenarios with brief inline comments explaining why, not what
- Maintain a test helper and fixture README describing available utilities
- Include examples for custom matchers in their definition file
- Document project-specific mock strategies and global setup requirements

Remember to leverage Jest's rich ecosystem, parallel execution, and built-in code coverage for fast, reliable, and maintainable unit testing.
